A data race occurs when two threads access the same memory location without synchronization. Consequences: corrupted data, crashes, undefined behavior.

Using std::mutex
std::mutex m;
int counter = 0;
void increment() {
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; ++i) {
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
++counter;
}
}


std::lock_guard represents a strict lexical ownership model: acquire once, release at scope exit. std::unique_lock represents a movable, stateful lock-ownership object whose 
ownership can be transferred, released, reacquired, and used with condition variables.


std::lock_guard:

#include <mutex>

std::mutex m;
int counter = 0;

void increment()
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);

    ++counter;

} // lock_guard destructor automatically unlocks m



Conceptually:

enter scope
    |
    | lock()
    |
    | critical section
    |
exit scope
    |
    | unlock()
    v


You cannot do:

lock.unlock();   // ❌
lock.lock();     // ❌

The lock lifetime is tied directly to the scope.


std::unique_lock-

#include <mutex>

std::mutex m;
int counter = 0;

void increment()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    ++counter;

} // unlock automatically


At first glance it looks identical.
But unique_lock can do much more:

void example()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    // Critical section

    lock.unlock();

    // Non-critical work

    lock.lock();

    // Critical section again
}


So its state can be:

unique_lock
    |
    +-- owns mutex
    |
    +-- does not own mutex
            |
            +-- lock() --> owns



| Feature                                                                  | `std::lock_guard` | `std::unique_lock` |
|---                                                                       |      ---          |                 ---|
| RAII                                                                     | Yes               | Yes                |
| Automatically unlocks                                                    | Yes               | Yes                |
| `lock()` manually                                                        | No                | Yes                |
| `unlock()` manually                                                      | No                | Yes                |
| Can defer locking                                                        | No                | Yes                |
| Can try-lock                                                             | No                | Yes                |
| Can transfer ownership                                                   | No                | Yes                |
| Works with `condition_variable`                                          | No                | **Yes**            |
| Movable                                                                  | No                | **Yes**            |
| Copyable                                                                 | No                | No                 |
| Complexity                                                               | Lower             | Higher             |
| Typical overhead                                                         | Minimal           | Slightly more state/logic |
| Best use                                                                 | Simple critical section | Complex lock lifetime |



Why unique_lock is necessary for condition_variable-

Consider a producer-consumer queue.

#include <condition_variable>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>

std::mutex m;
std::condition_variable cv;
std::queue<int> q;

void consumer()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    cv.wait(lock, [] {
        return !q.empty();
    });

    int value = q.front();
    q.pop();
}

The interesting part is:

cv.wait(lock, ...);

condition_variable::wait() needs to:
1. Atomically release the mutex.
2. Put the thread to sleep.
3. Wake the thread.
4. Reacquire the mutex.
5. Return to the caller.


Conceptually:

                mutex locked
                     |
                     v
              +-------------+
              | check queue  |
              +-------------+
                     |
                  empty?
                     |
                    YES
                     |
                     v
             unlock mutex
                     |
                     v
                   sleep
                     |
                notification
                     |
                     v
             wake up thread
                     |
                     v
              lock mutex again
                     |
                     v
              check predicate
                     |
                    YES
                     |
                     v
             consume element

unique_lock provides the ownership semantics required for this.


Why can't we simply use lock_guard?

This won't work:

void consumer()
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);

    cv.wait(lock, [] {
        return !q.empty();
    });
}

Because condition_variable::wait() requires a std::unique_lock<std::mutex>.
The fundamental reason is that wait() must be able to manipulate the mutex ownership.
lock_guard essentially says:
“I acquired this mutex at construction and I will hold it until destruction.”

unique_lock says:
“I currently own this mutex, but my ownership can temporarily be released and later reacquired.”

That's the deeper distinction.


defer_lock-
Another important unique_lock feature:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m, std::defer_lock);

// mutex is NOT locked yet

// do something...

lock.lock();

// critical section

lock.unlock();

With unique_lock:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m, std::defer_lock);
// mutex NOT locked
This becomes particularly useful when acquiring multiple mutexes.


Staff-level example: avoiding deadlock with multiple mutexes
Suppose:

std::mutex accountA;
std::mutex accountB;

Naive implementation:

void transfer()
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lockA(accountA);
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lockB(accountB);

    // transfer
}

Another thread might do:

void reverseTransfer()
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lockB(accountB);
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lockA(accountA);

    // transfer
}

You can get:

Thread 1                    Thread 2

lock(A)                     lock(B)
   |                           |
   |                           |
wait for B                wait for A
   |                           |
   +---------- DEADLOCK -------+


A better approach is:

void transfer()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lockA(accountA, std::defer_lock);
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lockB(accountB, std::defer_lock);

    std::lock(lockA, lockB);

    // Both mutexes locked safely
}

Or, when you don't need unique_lock afterward:

void transfer()
{
    std::scoped_lock lock(accountA, accountB);

    // Both locked
}

“Why use unique_lock here instead of lock_guard?”

“I need deferred ownership so I can acquire multiple mutexes using std::lock, which provides deadlock-avoidance semantics. If I only need simple simultaneous locking and no later 
ownership manipulation, std::scoped_lock is a cleaner C++17 choice.”

try_lock-

try_lock() attempts to acquire the mutex without blocking.

Returns true if the mutex was successfully locked.
Returns false if another thread already owns the mutex.
Unlike lock(), it does not wait for the mutex to become available.

unique_lock can also avoid blocking:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m, std::try_to_lock);

if (lock.owns_lock()) {
    // Successfully acquired mutex
    do_work();
} else {
    // Mutex was already held
    handle_busy_case();
}

This is useful when blocking isn't acceptable.

Thread A
   |
   | owns mutex
   |
   v

Thread B
   |
   | try_lock
   |
   +----> fails
           |
           v
      do something else


Ownership transfer
This is another major difference.
unique_lock is movable.

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> acquireLock()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
    return lock;
}


The ownership can be transferred:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock1(m);

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock2 = std::move(lock1);

After this:

lock1
  |
  | moved-from
  v
doesn't own mutex


lock2
  |
  v
owns mutex

This is conceptually similar to std::unique_ptr:
There can be only one active owner of the lock object.

That's where the name unique_lock comes from.


owns_lock()
Because unique_lock can exist without owning a mutex, it has state:

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m, std::defer_lock);

if (!lock.owns_lock()) {
    lock.lock();
}

if (lock.owns_lock()) {
    // mutex is currently owned
}

This is another fundamental difference.
A lock_guard has no useful “not currently owning the lock” state.

Early unlock
This is a common real-world reason to use unique_lock.
Suppose you have:

void process()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    update_shared_state();

    lock.unlock();

    expensive_operation();

}


You don't want to hold the mutex while doing:

expensive_operation();

This is important because holding locks for longer than necessary can cause:
- contention
- thread starvation
- lower throughput
- priority inversion
- increased latency



