Simple critical section-

std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);

Flexible lock management-

std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

lock.unlock();
...
lock.lock();

Multiple mutexes-

std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2, m3);

Which one is faster?

lock_guard generally has the simpler implementation because it only needs to acquire in construction and release in destruction. 
unique_lock maintains additional ownership state and supports deferred locking, manual unlock/relock, try-locking, and ownership transfer. 
Therefore, when those capabilities aren't needed, lock_guard communicates intent more precisely and may have less overhead. But I wouldn't choose 
between them based solely on microbenchmark performance—the synchronization strategy and contention dominate in most real systems.






